(1) Small scale SESs. Small scale SESs shall be considered as an accessory use in the zoned district subject to the following:
a. When located on the ground or attached to a framework located on the ground, SES panels shall not exceed 20 feet in height above the ground, or the height of any existing structure, whichever is lower.
b. Small scale SES panels shall conform to all setback requirements shown above.
c. Small scale SES panels shall not exceed 50 percent lot coverage or the maximum allowable coverage for the district in which they are located, whichever is less.
(2) Roof-mounted SES. Roof-mounted SES panels shall not project vertically more than the height requirements for the district in which they are located. The owner shall provide proof of notification to the managing authority of any airport located within five miles of the proposed location where the size of installation exceeds one acre. To ensure emergency personnel access, roof-mounted SESs shall provide access and spacing requirements in order to: (1) ensure roof access and egress; (2) provide pathways throughout the roof area; and (3) provide ventilation opportunity areas.
(3) Large scale SESs shall be ground mounted:
a. The large scale SES shall be located at distances as shown above.
(4) Buffers. A large scale SES shall have, to the extent reasonably practical, a complete visual buffer of natural vegetation, plantings, earth berms, and/or fencing that provides a reasonable visual and lighting screen to eliminate the view of the SES from residential dwelling units on adjacent lots, including lots located across a public right-of-way. The existing natural tree growth and natural landforms along the SES perimeter may create a sufficient buffer and shall be preserved when reasonably practicable. Any visual buffer must be established and maintained in accordance with the most recent visual buffer plan approved by the Lincoln County Planning and Zoning Board, Lincoln County Board of Commissioners, and as further described in the special use provision of this ordinance.
(5) Signage. A large scale SES:
1. Shall display signs (a) stating the risks that may result from contact with a large scale SES, (b) identifying the owner or operator of the large scale SES, and (c) providing a 24-hour contact telephone number;
2. Shall comply with the requirements of the applicable zoning district for displaying advertisement; and
3. May have signs that contain educational information about large scale SES.
(6) Special use permit. A ground mounted SES shall be allowed only by a special use permit solely within the Agricultural A-1 district. In addition to the general requirements for a special use permit application set forth in the county zoning code, the following shall be contained in any special use permit application for a ground mounted SES, to be considered by the planning and zoning commission and board of commissioners as additional criteria for their respective recommendation and decision:
a. Basic information. The applicant shall submit documentation that provides the following:
1. The address of the property on which the SES will be located;
2. The applicant's name, address, telephone number, and e-mail address;
3. The property owner's name, address, telephone number, and e-mail address;
4. If known, the SES operator's name, address, telephone number, e-mail address;
5. If known, the installation company's name, address, telephone number, e-mail address, and license number; and
6. Evidence of the applicant's control of the property, such as a deed, lease, or other agreement with the landowner.
b. Planning. The applicant shall submit the following:
Certifications. The applicant shall submit an affidavit that provides to the best of the applicant's knowledge:
1. One separate and individual surety bond for the construction and operation of the SES shall comply with all applicable federal and state laws. The amount of the bond shall be determined by the Lincoln County, Board of Commissioners;
2. Construction and operation of the SES will comply with all local laws, including the requirements of the Lincoln County zoning codes.
3. Additionally, one separate and individual commercial general liability insurance policy in an amount to be determined by the Lincoln County Board of Commissioners. Any bond shall be maintained throughout the siting, construction, installation, operation, and decommissioning of the SES.
A. A site plan of the property that depicts the locations of all existing and proposed structures (including solar rays, inverters, transformers, electrical substations, and buildings), property lines, rights-of-way, roads, required setbacks, and buffers;
B. A topographic map that depicts vegetative cover, watersheds, or wetlands on the property;
C. A soil erosion plan that adequately protects from erosion and runoff during construction and post construction;
D. A visual buffer plan that demonstrates that any (a) buffer will minimize impacts of the SES on adjacent residential dwelling units, as required by this ordinance, (b) that preserves natural tree growth and natural land forms along the SES perimeter, as required by this ordinance, and (c) adheres to any additional visual buffer requirements of Lincoln County zoning code that may further minimize impacts by the SES on the community character;
E. If the SES is located in an agricultural district, a map that identifies prime farmland and farmland of statewide importance on the property and a list that identifies (a) federal and state endangered, threatened, or candidate species that may be present on the property or within 1,000 feet of the property, and (b) critical habitat on the property or within 1,000 feet of the property;
F. The board of commissioners of Lincoln County shall review and determine if the SES de-commissioning plan is acceptable. Prior to any action being taken the de-commissioning plan must be approved by the Lincoln County Board of Commissioners. However, at a minimum the Lincoln County Board of Commissioners shall require the following:
(a) The name, address, telephone number, and e-mail address of the person(s) and/or entity(ies) responsible for implementing the de-commissioning plan. The plan shall also state that the property owner has been informed by the applicant or SES owner/operator of the de-commissioning responsibility and projected costs if that responsibility is allocated to the property owner by agreement between the SES owner/operator and property owner;
(b) A statement of conditions that require the de-commissioning plan to be implemented;
(c) A removal plan that identifies all structures, components, and non-utility owned equipment that shall be removed;
(d) Restoration plan to return the property to its condition prior to the installation of the SES or to some other condition reasonably appropriate for the intended land use after the SES is removed;
(e) The anticipated life of the SES and the estimated de-commissioning costs;
(f) One separate and individual surety bond in an amount sufficient, in the determination of the Lincoln County Board of Commissioners, to guarantee that funds will be available for the de-commissioning and restoration of the SES. All surety bonds shall be issued by a surety that is authorized by law to conduct surety business in the State of Georgia and listed in the most recent edition of the United States Department of the Treasury Circular 570 or any other appropriate federal rules and laws, as being approved as a surety in an amount no less than the amount of the surety bond required hereunder.
(i) The bond shall consist of three components for purposes of determining its amount. The first component will address environmental liabilities, including hazardous materials liabilities, such as risks associated with hazardous waste and hazardous substances. The second component will address de-commissioning, removal, and proper disposal of improvements and facilities. The third component will address reclamation, revegetation, restoration, and soil stabilization. There shall be a single instrument to cover all liabilities. The entire bond amount may be used to address a single risk event such as hazardous materials release or ground water contamination regardless of whether in calculating the total amount other risks were also considered. If the bond is used to address a particular risk, the holder would then be required to increase the bond amount to compensate for this use.
(ii) The surety bond shall remain in place as long as SES exists, regardless of whether it is actively operating. The surety bond shall be released if all work required under the de-commissioning plan is properly and timely executed. If the work required under the de-commissioning plan is not properly or timely executed, Lincoln County may at its sole discretion call or forfeit such surety bond and use the proceeds therefrom to execute the de-commissioning plan.
G. A plan that details a fire suppression system sufficient to prevent a fire from spreading off the site. This shall be accomplished by creating a nine feet firebreak with complete clearance of any combustible debris and vegetation at all times, and spreading gravel and fire-resistant underlayment or other fire-resistant material underneath any equipment likely to drop burning debris should a fire occur. A minimum six feet wide fire break composed of gravel or other fire-resistant material placed around the perimeter of the installation may also be used in lieu of, or in addition to the above.
H. Proof of notification to the managing authority of any airport located within five miles of the proposed location.
I. Any other relevant studies, reports, certificates and approvals as may be reasonably requested by Lincoln County, including, but not limited to, design review.
(Ord. of 5-9-2024)

684:
The regulations and requirements of this article are intended to:
(1) Protect the welfare, safety and integrity of residential neighborhoods and foster, through appropriate zoning and land use controls, a competitive environment for communication carriers that does not unreasonably discriminate among providers of functionally equivalent services and shall not prohibit or have the effect of prohibiting the provision of such services, so as to promote the county as a proactive community in the availability of communications services;
(2) Provide for the appropriate location and development of communication towers or antennas to serve the residents and businesses of the county;
(3) Minimize adverse visual effects of towers or antennas through careful design, siting and vegetative screening;
(4) Avoid potential damage to adjacent properties from tower or antenna failure through careful siting of tower or antenna structures;
(5) Maximize use of any new or existing communication tower or antenna to reduce the number of towers or antennas needed; and
(6) Encourage and promote the location of new communication facilities in areas which are not zoned for residential use.
(Ord. of 12-8-2016)

